          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Milk signifies elementary spiritual teaching or nourishment suited to new or immature believers, positively enabling growth but negatively indicating immaturity when believers remain dependent on it.\",\"book_summary\":\"Milk is the word in its initially received, uninterpreted state: suitable for babes but intended to be worked, tested, transformed, and eventually outgrown into 'meat.'\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Basic spiritual teaching or nourishment for spiritually immature believers.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The word prior to interpretation and transformative processing.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus classifies teaching by its elementary level and suitability for immature believers. The book instead classifies it by its unprocessed state before interpretation. It therefore may exclude elementary teaching that has already been interpreted and may classify any initially received word as milk until it is worked and tested.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no replacement of the general spiritual-word or teaching referent. The material difference concerns what makes it 'milk': elementary content in the consensus versus pre-interpretive reception in the book.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings retain milk as spiritual nourishment associated with babes and contrasted with meat. The book materially sharpens and partly reclassifies that category by making lack of interpretation or processing the defining condition and by presenting maturation as transformation of milk into butter/meat, rather than merely advancement from basic to deeper teaching. This is a boundary-level refinement, not a divergent referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited texts strongly support milk as the word or first principles for babes, but none explicitly identifies milk as the word 'before interpretation.' Proverbs 30:33 supports literal churning into butter, while the claim that butter is spiritually 'meat' and represents interpreted or tested word is an additional analogical inference rather than a directly stated scriptural identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "role": "assistant"
    }
